<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Songhai Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Songhai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Songhai Empire was a West African state centred on Gao on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>, which expanded in the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-ali">Sunni Ali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/askia-muhammad">Askia Muhammad</a>. It controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djenne">Djenné</a> and the trans-Saharan trade and became one of the largest states in African history. It was defeated by a Moroccan invasion at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tondibi">Battle of Tondibi</a> in 1591.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/songhai-empire.jpg" alt="Songhai Empire" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: HetmanTheResearcher · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Songhay_Empire.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In West Africa, the southern branch under Abu Bakr ibn Umar helped spread Maliki Islam through the Sahel, contributing to the Islamisation of the western Sudan and shaping the religious character of the later Mali and <b>Songhai</b> empires.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Kano paid tribute to the Kanem-Bornu Empire from about the mid-fifteenth century, and <b>Songhai</b> forces under Askia Muhammad probably campaigned in Hausaland around 1513, although the extent of Songhai control is debated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hausa-kingdoms">Hausa kingdoms · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Their trade linked Dagbon with the Fezzan, Egypt and the Bight of Benin, and the kingdom exchanged salt and kola nuts with the Mossi kingdoms, the Mali and <b>Songhai</b> empires and the Hausa Bakwai.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-dagbon">Kingdom of Dagbon · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its population included Mandinka, Soninke, Fulani, <b>Songhai</b> and Tuareg communities, fostering cultural exchange and binding peripheral regions into a coherent imperial economy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mali-empire">Mali Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After the conquest of <b>Songhai</b> in 1591, gold from the Niger valley arrived in Marrakesh, earning Ahmad al-Mansur the name al-Dhahabi, the Golden, though the flow fell short of his hopes within a few years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saadi-sultanate">Saadi Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…lived in a town of their own beside the capital of Ghana, and the rulers of Mali and <b>Songhai</b> accepted the faith; along the coast of East Africa it became the religion of the Swahili towns; in the Indian Ocean it spread to the ports of India and…</blockquote><a href="/religion/islam">Islam · Spread</a></li></ul></section>
